Presidential elections were held in [[Benin]] on 11 April 2021 to elect the [[President of Benin|President of the Republic of Benin]] for a [[List of political term limits|five-year term]]. Incumbent president [[Patrice Talon]] was re-elected for a second term in office with 86% of the vote.<ref name="results">{{Cite web|title=Bénin : Patrice Talon réélu dès le premier tour, selon la commission électorale|url=https://www.jeuneafrique.com/1154144/politique/benin-patrice-talon-reelu-des-le-premier-tour-selon-la-commission-electorale/|access-date=13 April 2021|website=JeuneAfrique}}</ref>

== Background ==
The [[2016 Beninese presidential election|2016 presidential elections]] saw the election of [[Patrice Talon]], who won in the second round of voting with nearly double the number of votes than his opponent [[Lionel Zinsou]], Prime Minister running under outgoing president [[Thomas Boni Yayi]]'s [[Cowry Forces for an Emerging Benin]] party. Benin has a reputation for being a stable democracy on the African continent.<ref name="participation">{{Cite journal|date=28 April 2019|title=Bénin : une abstention massive aux législatives pour protester contre le gouvernement|url=https://www.lemonde.fr/afrique/article/2019/04/28/benin-une-abstention-massive-aux-legislatives-pour-protester-contre-le-gouvernement_5456027_3212.html|journal=Le Monde|language=fr|access-date=1 May 2019}}</ref>

Upon becoming president, Talon undertook swift economic reforms aimed at liberalization. This provoked protests and strikes in the country and later led to the government tightening regulations on public protests, which led to accusations of [[authoritarianism]]. In parallel, the president has attempted to combat [[corruption]] in the country. During the presidential campaign, Talon highlighted [[good governance]] as one of his key themes, including the introduction of a single presidential term, which he called a "moral requirement". However, this reform was blocked by [[Parliament of Benin|Parliament]], who refused to create the precedent that a president could modify the constitutional terms of his mandate.

=== Political crisis ===
[[File:Burnt_cars_in_Cotonou,_1_May_2019.jpg|thumb|Burnt vehicles in [[Cotonou]], 1 May 2019]]

The approval and reputation of President Talon were seriously damaged in the wake of the [[2019 Beninese parliamentary election|2019 parliamentary elections]], which saw the government [[Democratic backsliding|democratically backslide]] by drastically restricting the eligibility of political parties, with only two pro-government parties able to run, excluding any opposition parties. The center-left [[Progressive Union (Benin)|Progressive Union]] and the centrist [[Republican Bloc]], the two pro-government parties created for the election, won all the seats in parliament, meaning no opposition movements would have a direct voice in legislative matters. Opposition parties called for a boycott of the election, which led to a record low 23% voter turnout, down from 65% in the previous election.<ref>{{Cite news|date=6 March 2019|title=Au Bénin, l’opposition privée d’élections législatives|language=fr|website=Le Monde|url=https://www.lemonde.fr/afrique/article/2019/03/06/au-benin-l-opposition-privee-d-elections-legislatives_5432270_3212.html|access-date=29 January 2021}}</ref>

The opposition again accused the president of authoritarianism and called for an end to the vote-counting process and the organization of new elections without success.<ref name="participation"/><ref>{{Cite web|date=29 April 2019|title=Bénin : sale temps pour Cotonou|url=https://www.lepoint.fr/afrique/benin-sale-temps-pour-cotonou-29-04-2019-2309896_3826.php|access-date=1 May 2019|website=Le Point|language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=30 April 2019|title=Législatives au Bénin : l’opposition lance un ultimatum à Patrice Talon au lendemain du scrutin|url=https://www.jeuneafrique.com/768960/politique/legislatives-au-benin-lopposition-lance-un-ultimatum-a-patrice-talon-au-lendemain-du-scrutin/|access-date=1 May 2019|website=Jeune Afrique|language=fr}}</ref> Two days after the election, [[Amnesty International]] released a statement denouncing "the wave of arbitrary arrests of political activists and journalists and the repression of peaceful protests".<ref>{{Cite web|date=29 April 2019|title=Bénin : en l'absence de l'opposition, abstention massive aux législatives|url=https://www.france24.com/fr/20190429-benin-absence-opposition-abstention-massive-legislatives-patrice-talon|access-date=1 May 2019|website=France 24|language=fr}}</ref> The election was also marked by a complete [[Internet shutdown]] on the day of the vote, an action criticized by the opposition, [[civil society]] and international observers, the level of repression on the part of the government was described as "alarming".<ref>{{Cite web|title=Bénin. Couper Internet le jour du scrutin est une attaque directe contre la liberté d’expression|url=https://www.amnesty.org/fr/latest/news/2019/04/benin-internet-shutdown-on-election-day-is-a-blunt-attack/|access-date=1 May 2019|website=www.amnesty.org|language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Les résultats des législatives espérés jeudi|url=https://www.voaafrique.com/a/législatives-le-bénin-toujours-dans-l-attente-des-résultats/4897710.html|access-date=1 May 2019|website=VOA|language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=30 April 2019|title=Législatives: le Bénin toujours dans l'attente des résultats|url=https://information.tv5monde.com/afrique/legislatives-le-benin-toujours-dans-l-attente-des-resultats-297865|access-date=1 May 2019|website=TV5 Monde|language=fr|archive-date=1 May 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190501003915/https://information.tv5monde.com/afrique/legislatives-le-benin-toujours-dans-l-attente-des-resultats-297865|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="prelimi">{{Cite web|title=Législatives au Bénin: la Commission électorale confirme la faible participation|url=http://www.rfi.fr/afrique/20190501-legislatives-benin-commission-electorale-faible-participation|access-date=1 May 2019|website=RFI Afrique|language=fr}}</ref> The days following the election involved violent clashes between protesters and security forces, with the latter firing [[tear gas]] and live ammunition, while protesters [[burning tire]]s and throwing various objects.<ref name="retour">{{Cite web|date=2 May 2019|title=Retour au calme au Bénin après les violences post-électorales|url=https://www.france24.com/fr/20190502-benin-cotonou-residence-boni-yayi-tensions-talon-legislatives|access-date=17 May 2019|website=France 24|language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=2 May 2019|title=Bénin : heurts autour du domicile de Boni Yayi|url=https://information.tv5monde.com/video/benin-heurts-autour-du-domicile-de-boni-yayi|access-date=17 May 2019|website=TV5MONDE|language=fr}}</ref> Amnesty International denounced the death of four protesters as well as continued arbitrary arrests, which led to the serious injury of another protester. A strong security contingent was deployed on the streets of [[Cotonou]] and [[Porto-Novo]] leading up to the [[Investiture|investiture ceremony]] for the new Assembly. [[Parachute Commandos (Benin)|Para Commandos]], [[Riot control vehicle|anti-riot vehicles]], the CRS and units of the [[Republican guard|Republican Guard]] were all deployed to maintain order.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Bénin: installation de la nouvelle législature sous haute sécurité|url=http://www.rfi.fr/afrique/20190515-benin-installation-nouvelle-legislature-sous-haute-securite|access-date=17 May 2019|website=RFI Afrique|language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Bénin: le ministre de l'Intérieur reconnaît l'usage d'armes par la police|url=http://www.rfi.fr/afrique/20190502-benin-ministre-interieur-sacca-lafia-boni-yayi-armes-letales-enquete|access-date=17 May 2019|website=RFI Afrique|language=fr}}</ref>

Nevertheless, a six-month political dialogue process led to an agreement on rules regarding electoral participation. During the 2020 municipal elections, only five political parties were validated by the central election authority, down from 34 parties and alliances in the 2015 municipal elections.<ref>{{Cite web|year=2020|title=[Édito] Les municipales au Bénin, un test démocratique – Jeune Afrique|url=https://www.jeuneafrique.com/mag/912820/politique/edito-les-municipales-au-benin-un-test-democratique/|access-date=20 April 2020|website=Jeune Afrique}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|year=2020|title=Elections communales et municipales de mai 2020: L’ordre de passage des partis sur les médias validé - La Nation Bénin|url=https://lanationbenin.info/elections-communales-et-municipales-de-mai-2020-lordre-de-passage-des-partis-sur-les-medias-valide/|access-date=20 April 2020|website=La Nation Bénin|archive-date=5 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210205132458/https://lanationbenin.info/elections-communales-et-municipales-de-mai-2020-lordre-de-passage-des-partis-sur-les-medias-valide/|url-status=dead}}</ref> The [[Cowry Forces for an Emerging Benin]] was the only opposition party to exceed the 10% vote threshold needed for representation.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Les résultats des élections municipales du 17 mai 2020|url=https://www.24haubenin.info/?Les-resultats-des-elections-municipales-du-17-mai-2020|access-date=24 May 2020|website=24 Hau Benin}}</ref> This has led to some international observers questioning the strength of democracy in the country, which had previously been considered one of the more stable in Africa, due to an increasing restrictive and authoritarian bent, a loss of political pluralism, and key political rivals of President Talon either in exile or excluded from political participation.<ref>{{Cite web|year=2020|title=Au Bénin, quatre ans après l’élection de Patrice Talon, la dérive autoritaire|url=https://www.lemonde.fr/afrique/article/2020/05/15/quatre-ans-apres-l-election-de-patrice-talon-la-derive-autoritaire-du-benin_6039743_3212.html|access-date=30 December 2020|website=Le Monde|issn=1950-6244}}</ref><ref name="parrainages">{{Cite web|year=2020|title=Au Bénin, une démocratie qui chancelle et inquiète, à cinq mois de la présidentielle|url=https://www.lemonde.fr/afrique/article/2020/11/24/au-benin-une-democratie-qui-chancelle-et-inquiete-a-cinq-mois-de-la-presidentielle_6060967_3212.html|access-date=30 December 2020|website=Le Monde|issn=1950-6244}}</ref>

=== Preparation for the 2021 elections ===
As the 2021 elections approached, the incumbent president was seen as theoretically the only person able to run as a candidate. A 2019 revision to the constitution imposed a requirement that candidates need receive the nomination of 10% of the total MPs and mayors (about 16 sponsors). However, 160 of the electoral officials (83 Members of Parliament and 77 mayors) concerned belong to pro-government parties.<ref name="parrainages"/><ref name="parrainagesA">{{Cite web|year=2020|title=Présidentielle 2021 au Bénin: "Il n'y a aucun risque d'exclusion", Alain Orounla|url=https://beninwebtv.com/2020/12/presidentielle-2021-au-benin-il-ny-a-aucun-risque-dexclusion-alain-orounla/|access-date=30 December 2020|website=Bénin Web TV|archive-date=23 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210123010012/https://beninwebtv.com/2020/12/presidentielle-2021-au-benin-il-ny-a-aucun-risque-dexclusion-alain-orounla/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Only six mayors belonging to the Cowry Forces for an Emerging Benin make up the opposition, with the party lacking a Member of Parliament.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Parrainage au Bénin : vers une élection présidentielle exclusive ?|url=https://www.afrik.com/parrainage-au-benin-vers-une-election-presidentielle-exclusive|access-date=7 January 2021|website=Afrik}}</ref> President Talon promised to "do everything" to organize an inclusive, open ballot for his opponents to obtain nomination, a move seen by opposition parties as the president's way to "cherry pick" his challengers.<ref>{{Cite web|year=2020|title=Présidentielle au Bénin: la main tendue de Patrice Talon à l'opposition|url=https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20201127-pr%C3%A9sidentielle-au-b%C3%A9nin-la-main-tendue-de-patrice-talon-%C3%A0-l-opposition|access-date=30 December 2020|website=RFI|publisher=RFI}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Bénin : La Présidentielle fixée au 11 April 2021, Patrice Talon promet de faire participer l'opposition|url=https://www.koaci.com/index.php/article/2020/11/26/benin/Politique/benin-la-presidentielle-fixee-au-11-avril-2021-patrice-talon-promet-de-faire-participer-lopposition_146955.html|access-date=30 December 2020|website=KOACI}}</ref> The Cowry Forces for an Emerging Benin, as well as the Democrats (a new party created by supporters of former president [[Boni Yayi]]) fear they will be designated as trojan horses by President Talon.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Présidentielle 2021 : "Patrice Talon va désigner un candidate pour Les Démocrates"|url=https://www.banouto.info/article/politique/20201125-presidentielle-2021-patrice-talon-va-designer-un-candidat-pour-les-democrates|access-date=30 December 2020|website=Banouto}}</ref> The leader of the Democrats, [[Reckya Madougou]], was arrested on 3 March 2021 and charged with "financing terrorism".<ref>{{Cite web|date=2021-03-08|title=Benin opposition candidate jailed and accused of terrorism|url=https://www.africanews.com/2021/03/08/benin-opposition-candidate-jailed-and-accused-of-terrorism/|url-status=live|access-date=2021-03-09|website=Africanews|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210308221725/https://www.africanews.com/2021/03/08/benin-opposition-candidate-jailed-and-accused-of-terrorism/ |archive-date=8 March 2021 }}</ref>

=== Protests ===
In April 2021 protests occurred in several Beninese cities in light of Talon's decision to stand for re-election after initially stating he would only serve for one term. Demonstrations were noted to have occurred in the commercial capital, Cotonou, as well as the city of Parakou in the centre of the country.<ref>{{Cite news|date=6 April 2021|title=Protests spread in Benin as president readies for re-election bid|language=en|work=Reuters|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-benin-election-protests-idUSKBN2BT269|access-date=6 April 2021}}</ref> On 8 April 2021 it was reported that two protestors had been shot dead and five more injured by security forces in [[Savè]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Benin election protest death toll rises to two|url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/world/benin-election-protest-death-toll-rises-to-two/ar-BB1fsYZg|access-date=2021-04-09|website=www.msn.com}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
The president of Benin is elected by a [[two-round system]] to a five-year term that is renewable once. If no candidate receives a majority of the vote in the first round, the top-two candidates compete in a second round organized within fifteen days of the first round.<ref name="constitution">{{Cite web|title=Bénin, Constitution du 11 décembre 1990, MJP|url=https://mjp.univ-perp.fr/constit/bj1990.htm|access-date=30 December 2020|website=Digithèque MJP}}</ref> After a constitutional amendment adopted in November 2019, each presidential candidate has to designate a [[Vice President of Benin|vice-presidential running mate]]. The vice president also has a five-year term and is charged with completing the president's term in case of impeachment or other impediment.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Le Bénin se dote d'un vice-président|url=https://www.dw.com/fr/le-b%C3%A9nin-se-dote-dun-vice-pr%C3%A9sident/a-51101607|access-date=9 January 2021|website=Deutsche Welle}}</ref>

Candidates for the presidency have to be a [[Beninese]] citizen, either [[Jus soli|by birth]] or from having lived in Benin for the past ten years, be of "good character and great probity", have all their civil and political rights, be between the ages of 40 and 70, and submit to a physical and mental assessment by three doctors sworn in by the Constitutional Court.<ref name="constitution"/> A 2019 constitutional amendment also requires candidates to be nominated by no less than 10% of the total of Members of Parliament and Mayors, or about 16 officials.<ref name="parrainagesA"/>

== Candidates ==
Candidates have until 4 February to obtain the nominations necessary and announce their candidacy.<ref name="calendrier">{{Cite web|title=Présidentielle 2021 au Bénin : la CENA dévoile le calendrier électoral|url=https://www.afrik.com/presidentielle-2021-au-benin-la-cena-devoile-le-calendrier-electoral|access-date=7 January 2021|website=Afrik}}</ref> Twenty people, including incumbent President Patrice Talon, enrolled as candidates.<ref name=femalevp>{{cite news |title=Benin's president Talon files candidacy for April vote |url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/world/benins-president-talon-files-candidacy-for-april-vote/ar-BB1dqiCV?ocid=ob-fb-enus-1541512262291 |access-date=5 February 2021 |work=msn.com |agency=AFP |date=5 February 2021}}</ref>

Law professor Frédéric Joël Aïvo was the first to declare his candidacy.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Présidentielle au Bénin : Joël Aïvo ira " jusqu’au bout "|url=https://www.jeuneafrique.com/1104502/politique/togo-joel-aivo-trop-independant/|website=JeuneAfrique|language=fr}}</ref> Several opposition leaders have supported a coalition backing Aïvo.<ref name=femalevp/> President Talon announced his intention to run for another term on 15 January 2021, breaking his 2016 promise to run for one term.<ref name=ML1>{{Cite web|title=Présidentielle 2021/ Flou à propos du colistier du chef de l’État: Le Vice-président, un choix apparemment difficile pour Talon et sa mouvance|url=https://matinlibre.com/2021/01/19/presidentielle-2021-flou-a-propos-du-colistier-du-chef-de-letat-le-vice-president-un-choix-apparemment-difficile-pour-talon-et-sa-mouvance/|access-date=21 January 2021|website=Matin Libre|archive-date=19 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210119065354/https://matinlibre.com/2021/01/19/presidentielle-2021-flou-a-propos-du-colistier-du-chef-de-letat-le-vice-president-un-choix-apparemment-difficile-pour-talon-et-sa-mouvance/|url-status=dead}}</ref> The next week, Talon revealed his running-mate, Vice President of the Assembly [[Mariam Chabi Talata]]. The choice came as a surprise to observers, as Talata was not one of the names advanced by the pro-government parties and the president chose an alternative when the two parties were unable to agree.<ref name=ML1/><ref>{{Cite web|title=Présidentielle au Bénin: Talon choisit la vice-présidente de l'Assemblée comme colistière|url=https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20210124-pr%C3%A9sidentielle-au-b%C3%A9nin-talon-choisit-la-vice-pr%C3%A9sidente-de-l-assembl%C3%A9e-comme-colisti%C3%A8re|access-date=24 January 2021|website=RFI}}</ref> Talata would be Benin's first female vice president if elected.<ref name=femalevp/>

==Results==
Provisional results from the Autonomous National Electoral Commission (CENA) were released on 13 April showing that incumbent president [[Patrice Talon]] was re-elected with 86.4% of the vote.<ref name="results"/> Provisional results from the Constitutional Court on 15 April showed Talon winning with 87.3% of the vote.<ref>[https://courconstitutionnelle.bj/download/proclamation-des-resultats-provisoires-de-lelection-presidentielle-du-11-avril-2021/ Proclamation des résultats provisoires de l’élection présidentielle du 11 avril 2021] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210416162113/https://courconstitutionnelle.bj/download/proclamation-des-resultats-provisoires-de-lelection-presidentielle-du-11-avril-2021/ |date=16 April 2021 }} Cour Constitutionelle</ref> The final results were certified by the Constitutional Court on 20 April.<ref>{{cite web|title=Proclamation des résultats définitifs de l’élection présidentielle du 11 avril 2021|url=https://courconstitutionnelle.bj/proclamation-des-resultats-definitifs-de-lelection-presidentielle-du-11-avril-2021/|website=courconstitutionnelle.bj|access-date=24 April 2021|publication-date=|archive-date=24 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210424030855/https://courconstitutionnelle.bj/proclamation-des-resultats-definitifs-de-lelection-presidentielle-du-11-avril-2021/|url-status=dead}}.</ref>

